WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.969
A graph collects all input-output pairs of a rule

00:00:03.969 --> 00:00:09.521
Begin with a few easy inputs for y equals negative two x plus three

00:00:09.521 --> 00:00:15.867
When x is zero, one, and two, the corresponding outputs are three, one, and negative one.

00:00:17.167 --> 00:00:21.844
The constant rate shows that these points belong to one straight line

00:00:21.844 --> 00:00:26.912
Starting at the vertical intercept three, each step right goes two units down

00:00:26.912 --> 00:00:31.200
Drawing the line extends the relationship beyond the few sampled inputs.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:39.438
The horizontal intercept is found by setting the output to zero, not by guessing a plotted point

00:00:39.438 --> 00:00:41.478
Solving gives input three halves

00:00:41.478 --> 00:00:47.600
This is where the line crosses the horizontal axis, while its vertical intercept remains three.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.331
Graphing from samples also has limits

00:00:51.331 --> 00:00:59.433
These values come from a quadratic rule, so connecting them with a single straight line would invent a different relationship

00:00:59.433 --> 00:01:04.700
Use the formula, its structure, and enough points to determine the appropriate shape.
